empt failed because `CODEX_HOME` must exist. The stub had hidden that, so I added `mkdir \"$SH/.codex\"`. After the fix, Codex starts, writes its state into the scratch home, and produces no \"Operation not permitted\" lines. It then fails at the OpenAI auth handshake with a 401.\n\n## What I could not verify\n\n- **No end-to-end smoke run with a real answer.** `CODEX_API_KEY` is not set in this environment, and the `codex` lane here logs in from `~/.codex/auth.json`. The sandboxed lane therefore has never produced a graded answer. The first real run needs `CODEX_API_KEY` set, for example `CODEX_API_KEY=... ./run.sh fizz codex-sandboxed 1`.\n- **`OPENAI_BASE_URL` was ignored** by this Codex version. My dummy-key test therefore reached `api.openai.com`, and the 401 came back before any prompt was sent. I did not use a real key for it.\n\n## Differences from the `codex` lane, and trade-offs\n\n- **Auth is an environment variable, not a file.** The sandbox blocks file reads under `$HOME`, but the agent's shell still inherits `CODEX_API_KEY` from the environment and could read it. Keeping the auth file out of reach would mean keeping the key out of the environment as well. That is not possible while Codex needs it to log in, so this lane does not fully protect the key.\n- **Codex runs with its defaults.** The real `~/.codex/config.toml` pins no model or provider, so the default model applies in both lanes. It also defines MCP servers, which the sandboxed run does not load. That keeps the sandboxed run from reaching anything through MCP, but it is still a difference from the `codex` lane.\n- **The sandbox denies process execution only for three binaries, and `codex` is in `/opt/homebrew`, so it starts.** If Codex or Node were ever installed under `$HOME` (for example, under nvm), the sandbox would block them and the lane would fail. The real-binary test above shows this is not the case here.\n\n## Other notes\n\n- `/tmp/chome-UT8cZY` was there before I started (timestamp